IwOTMxMmM0NzI0MjQxNDhhNzIzZmI3YTY","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/MadiroGlobalHealth/openmrs-esm-lime/commit/bc786daf8509c83eb91135c9694a03451da16c51","sha":"bc786daf8509c83eb91135c9694a03451da16c51","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/MadiroGlobalHealth/openmrs-esm-lime/commits/bc786daf8509c83eb91135c9694a03451da16c51"}],"sha":"8262d61202a142cb09312c472424148a723fb7a6","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/MadiroGlobalHealth/openmrs-esm-lime/commits/8262d61202a142cb09312c472424148a723fb7a6"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/15266028?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/pirupius/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/pirupius/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/pirupius/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/pirupius/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/pirupius","id":15266028,"login":"pirupius","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE1MjY2MDI4","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/pirupius/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/pirupius/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/pirupius/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/pirupius/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/pirupius/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/pirupius","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/MadiroGlobalHealth/openmrs-esm-lime/commits/bc786daf8509c83eb91135c9694a03451da16c51/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-03T09:12:50Z","email":"piruville@gmail.com","name":"Pius Rubangakene"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-03T09:12:50Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"(chore) Migrate frontend modules to @openmrs/esm-framework 10.x (#51)\n\n* (chore) Migrate frontend modules to @openmrs/esm-framework 10.x\n\nThe LIME-EMR distro app shell now ships framework 10.x, but all three\npackages declared a 9.x peer dependency. Module Federation logged the\nsingleton mismatch and handed the apps v10 anyway, crashing the home\ndashboard with \"Cannot read properties of undefined (reading ''map'')\".\n\nExtensionSlotState dropped `assignedExtensions` in v10, replacing it\nwith the unfiltered `candidateExtensions`. Swap `useExtensionStore` for\nthe `useAssignedExtensions` hook, which is the reactive equivalent of\nwhat v9 returned: it applies display conditions. It always returns an\narray, so the `|| []` fallback is dropped.\n\nAlso bump @openmrs/esm-form-engine-lib, which is bundled into the\nnutrition app and was resolved at a build peering on framework 9.x.\nesm-patient-common-lib picks up an equivalent build within its existing\n12.x range via the lockfile.\n\n* (chore) Untrack Playwright MCP scratch output\n\nFour timestamped accessibility-tree dumps and a screenshot were swept\ninto unrelated feature PRs (#42, #43) by a blanket `git add`. They are\nbrowser-session scratch files, one of which snapshots a GitHub page,\nand nothing references them.\n\n* (chore) Make the e2e distro assembly fail loudly\n\n`openmrs assemble` exits 0 when it resolves no frontend modules, so a\nbroken spa-assemble-config.json produced a distro containing none of our\napps. The suite then ran against it and failed on missing headings,\npointing at the widgets rather than at the assembly that omitted them.\n\nPrint the generated config and exit non-zero when it resolves no\nmodules. Also set strict mode in the script body: flags passed as\n`env bash -eu` are not applied portably, so the script had been running\nwithout them.\n\n* (chore) Verify the assembled e2e SPA is not empty\n\nThe previous run showed a valid 5-module config still producing a distro\nwith none of our apps in it, while the same config and CLI version\nassemble correctly in an identical container locally. Print the\nassembled SPA and fail the build when it has no importmap, so the\nDocker build reports the problem instead of the test assertions.\n\n* (chore) Report what the running e2e frontend actually serves\n\nThe Docker build now proves it assembles an importmap containing our\nthree apps, yet the running frontend serves the upstream 47-app\nimportmap, so the tests exercise a distro without the widgets under\ntest. Inspect the live container after compose brings it up to show\nwhich of the two is actually being served.\n\n* (fix) Serve our assembled importmap in the e2e distro\n\nThe reference application image ships pre-compressed .br/.gz siblings\nbeside its assets and nginx prefers those over the plain file.\n`openmrs assemble` writes only uncompressed files, so our importmap.json\nand routes.registry.json sat on disk while nginx served the stale\nupstream copies. The suite therefore ran against a distro containing 47\nupstream apps and none of ours, and the mental-health and nutrition\nwidgets could never render.\n\nThe sizes nginx logged, 739 and 9166 bytes, are exactly the upstream\nimportmap.json.br and routes.registry.json.br, while the files on disk\nwere our 534 and 8472 byte versions.\n\nDelete any compressed sibling whose plain file is present, after the\nassembled SPA is copied in. Verified locally: a request sending\n`Accept-Encoding: br` now returns our 5-entry importmap with all three\n@madiro apps, where it previously returned the 47-entry upstream one.","tree":{"sha":"b490710ffce61ae38699cab0c169473452748e38","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/MadiroGlobalHealth/openmrs-esm-lime/git/trees/b490710ffce61ae38699cab0c169473452748e38"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/MadiroGlobalHealth/openmrs-esm-lime/git/commits/bc786daf8509c83eb91135c9694a03451da16c51","verification":{"payload":"tree b490710ffce61ae38699cab0c169473452748e38\nparent 8c29fa1667cef532c3fbdaadd89fbac2d0b3566f\nauthor Pius Rubangakene <piruville@gmail.com> 1788426770 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1788426770 -0700\n\n(chore) Migrate frontend modules to @openmrs/esm-framework 10.x (#51)\n\n* (chore) Migrate frontend modules to @openmrs/esm-framework 10.x\n\nThe LIME-EMR distro app shell now ships framework 10.x, but all three\npackages declared a 9.x peer dependency. Module Federation logged the\nsingleton mismatch and handed the apps v10 anyway, crashing the home\ndashboard with \"Cannot read properties of undefined (reading ''map'')\".\n\nExtensionSlotState dropped `assignedExtensions` in v10, replacing it\nwith the unfiltered `candidateExtensions`. Swap `useExtensionStore` for\nthe `useAssignedExtensions` hook, which is the reactive equivalent of\nwhat v9 returned: it applies display conditions. It always returns an\narray, so the `|| []` fallback is dropped.\n\nAlso bump @openmrs/esm-form-engine-lib, which is bundled into the\nnutrition app and was resolved at a build peering on framework 9.x.\nesm-patient-common-lib picks up an equivalent build within its existing\n12.x range via the lockfile.\n\n* (chore) Untrack Playwright MCP scratch output\n\nFour timestamped accessibility-tree dumps and a screenshot were swept\ninto unrelated feature PRs (#42, #43) by a blanket `git add`. They are\nbrowser-session scratch files, one of which snapshots a GitHub page,\nand nothing references them.\n\n* (chore) Make the e2e distro assembly fail loudly\n\n`openmrs assemble` exits 0 when it resolves no frontend modules, so a\nbroken spa-assemble-config.json produced a distro containing none of our\napps. The suite then ran against it and failed on missing headings,\npointing at the widgets rather than at the assembly that omitted them.\n\nPrint the generated config and exit non-zero when it resolves no\nmodules. Also set strict mode in the script body: flags passed as\n`env bash -eu` are not applied portably, so the script had been running\nwithout them.\n\n* (chore) Verify the assembled e2e SPA is not empty\n\nThe previous run showed a valid 5-module config still producing a distro\nwith none of our apps in it, while the same config and CLI version\nassemble correctly in an identical container locally. Print the\nassembled SPA and fail the build when it has no importmap, so the\nDocker build reports the problem instead of the test assertions.\n\n* (chore) Report what the running e2e frontend actually serves\n\nThe Docker build now proves it assembles an importmap containing our\nthree apps, yet the running frontend serves the upstream 47-app\nimportmap, so the tests exercise a distro without the widgets under\ntest. Inspect the live container after compose brings it up to show\nwhich of the two is actually being served.\n\n* (fix) Serve our assembled importmap in the e2e distro\n\nThe reference application image ships pre-compressed .br/.gz siblings\nbeside its assets and nginx prefers those over the plain file.\n`openmrs assemble` writes only uncompressed files, so our importmap.json\nand routes.registry.json sat on disk while nginx served the stale\nupstream copies. The suite therefore ran against a distro containing 47\nupstream apps and none of ours, and the mental-health and nutrition\nwidgets could never render.\n\nThe sizes nginx logged, 739 and 9166 bytes, are exactly the upstream\nimportmap.json.br and routes.registry.json.br, while the files on disk\nwere our 534 and 8472 byte versions.\n\nDelete any compressed sibling whose plain file is present, after the\nassembled SPA is copied in. Verified locally: a request sending\n`Accept-Encoding: br` now returns our 5-entry importmap with all three\n@madiro apps, where it previously returned the 47-entry upstream one.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqmToSCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAz24QAAU1rb7SLW/Craga0cMNnPPJ\nHstvFulg8AqG0QlyYorhl5BwrF4nINILc5FKuByYz8Zf9pd1ePw0rjpBEDaCdSGJ\n02MQVsI8aAIYB2f24dkwtmPo8i2QMGrpCif2vnOYUONJtvKZb/njeKMa4IEpvzHN\nmLins0mTXk+lwVVAE7of3aFSFo2iKna3cEmrBq5AZF5USJfJkxl6lJnj5owS8L8u\nxatBMxgbZt3D4H867Lw7Q5GIkxT+QLPdiCiSMSzNhV2f87QHxUCgSHkcj5h/Fr4Y\nwoKkiiSF7